      "text": "To solve the equation \\(7^{x+7} = 8^x\\), we start by taking the natural logarithm (or any logarithm) of both sides to simplify the exponents. Let's use the natural logarithm (ln) for this purpose.\n\n\\[\n\\ln(7^{x+7}) = \\ln(8^x)\n\\]\n\nUsing the property of logarithms that \\(\\ln(a^b) = b \\ln(a)\\), we can rewrite the equation as:\n\n\\[\n(x+7) \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nNext, we distribute the logarithms on both sides:\n\n\\[\nx \\ln 7 + 7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8\n\\]\n\nTo isolate \\(x\\), we subtract \\(x \\ln 7\\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n7 \\ln 7 = x \\ln 8 - x \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nWe can factor out \\(x\\) on the right side:\n\n\\[\n7 \\ln 7 = x (\\ln 8 - \\ln 7)\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\(x\\), we divide both sides by \\(\\ln 8 - \\ln 7\\):\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 8 - \\ln 7}\n\\]\n\nWe are given that the solution can be expressed in the form \\(x = \\log_b 7^7\\). Recall that \\(\\log_b a = \\frac{\\ln a}{\\ln b}\\). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\nx = \\log_b 7^7 = \\frac{\\ln 7^7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions are equal to \\(x\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 8 - \\ln 7} = \\frac{\\ln 7^7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nSimplify \\(\\ln 7^7\\):\n\n\\[\n\\ln 7^7 = 7 \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln 8 - \\ln 7} = \\frac{7 \\ln 7}{\\ln b}\n\\]\n\nSince the numerators are the same, we can equate the denominators:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln 8 - \\ln 7\n\\]\n\nUsing the properties of logarithms again, we can combine the logarithms on the right side:\n\n\\[\n\\ln b = \\ln \\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\(b = \\frac{8}{7}\\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{8}{7}}\n\\]",
